version: 2
title: Claude configuration dimensions
description: Where Claude runs, who owns a piece of configuration, how far it applies, what kind of thing it is, and when it enters context. Each event explains one decision's answers.
decisions:
  - key: harness
    label: Where does Claude run?
    values:
      - key: chat
        label: Chat app
        detail: claude.ai on the web or in the desktop app, including what Cowork did before the merge
        activates: [anchor]
      - key: code
        label: Claude Code
        detail: One engine in several hosts
        activates: [host]
  - key: anchor
    label: What is the chat anchored to?
    values:
      - key: none
        label: Nothing
      - key: project
        label: A Project
      - key: folder
        label: A folder
  - key: host
    label: Which host runs Claude Code?
    values:
      - key: terminal
        label: Terminal
      - key: desktop
        label: Desktop app, Code tab
      - key: ide
        label: VS Code or JetBrains extension
      - key: cloud
        label: Cloud session
  - key: owner
    label: Who owns it?
    values:
      - key: org
        label: Org
      - key: team
        label: Team
      - key: me
        label: Me
  - key: reach
    label: Where does it apply?
    values:
      - key: everywhere
        label: Everywhere
      - key: tree
        label: A folder and everything below it
      - key: project
        label: One Project
        when: [harness=chat]
  - key: kind
    label: What kind of thing is it?
    values:
      - key: instruction
        label: Instruction
      - key: rule
        label: Rule
        when: [harness=code]
      - key: skill
        label: Skill
      - key: memory
        label: Memory
      - key: knowledge
        label: Knowledge files
        when: [harness=chat]
      - key: settings
        label: Settings
  - key: load
    label: When does it enter context?
    values:
      - key: start
        label: At session start
      - key: path
        label: When a matching path is touched
      - key: invoke
        label: When invoked
      - key: open
        label: When Claude opens it
events:
  - key: harness
    label: The harness
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Where it runs
    content:
      key: harness
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Where Claude runs
        description: The outermost dimension. It decides which of the other questions apply at all.
        sections:
          - title: Chat app
            description: claude.ai on the web or in the desktop app. What Cowork did is now a chat anchored to a folder.
            body: |
              Reads from the account: Settings > General for standing instructions, Settings > Memory, Settings > Skills, and the connectors you enabled. Anchoring the chat to a Project or a folder adds that anchor's sources on top. Nothing on this PC is read unless the chat is anchored to a folder.

              Reach here means the whole account, one Project, or the anchored folder. Rules do not exist in this harness.
          - title: Claude Code
            description: One engine in the terminal, the desktop app's Code tab, an IDE extension or a cloud session.
            body: |
              Reads files from disk at session start: the CLAUDE.md chain from the home folder down through every ancestor to the working directory, the rules folders, the skills folders, the settings tiers and the auto-memory index. Path-scoped rules, subfolder CLAUDE.md files, skill bodies and memory topic files wait for their trigger.

              Reach here is always a folder and everything below it, with everywhere meaning the home folder. Claude Code is always anchored to a folder, its working directory.
          - title: What crosses the two
            body: |
              Skills. Uploaded on claude.ai, they sync down to `~\.claude\skills\synced` and appear in Claude Code as one group. Nothing else does: instructions, rules, memory and settings each live in one harness.
  - key: anchor
    label: The anchor
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Where it runs
    when: [harness=chat]
    content:
      key: anchor
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What a chat is anchored to
        description: Picked when the chat starts. It decides what reach means in the chat app.
        sections:
          - title: Nothing
            body: |
              Account-wide sources only: Settings > General, Settings > Memory, Settings > Skills, connectors. A probe should show only the account-level tokens.
          - title: A Project
            body: |
              The account sources plus the Project's instructions field, its knowledge files and its own memory space. A shared Project is the team's; a private one is mine. A probe should show the Project's token and nothing from disk.
          - title: A folder
            body: |
              The account sources plus file access to the folder. Three things are still to verify after the merge: whether the folder's CLAUDE.md is read, whether the old per-folder instructions field survived, and whether a memory saved here lands in the account or stays on this PC.
  - key: host
    label: The host
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Where it runs
    when: [harness=code]
    content:
      key: host
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Which host runs Claude Code
        description: The host changes the tools on offer, not where instructions come from. The cloud is the one exception.
        sections:
          - title: Terminal
            body: |
              The plain CLI: the files, the built-in tools, and the MCP servers named in settings and `.mcp.json`. `/context` prints which CLAUDE.md, rules and memory loaded; `/memory` opens them.
          - title: Desktop app, Code tab
            body: |
              The same files, plus the app's own servers: a browser pane, a terminal pane, session and sidebar controls. Probe sessions from the terminal and from this tab saw the same eleven tokens, which is the evidence that the host does not change the sources.
          - title: VS Code or JetBrains extension
            body: |
              The same files, plus the editor's context: diagnostics, the current selection, diffs shown in the editor.
          - title: Cloud session
            body: |
              Runs on another machine. The repo's committed files travel: its CLAUDE.md, `.claude\rules`, `.claude\skills`, `.claude\settings.json`. Nothing from `~\.claude` on this PC does: no user CLAUDE.md, user rules, user skills or auto-memory.
  - key: owner
    label: The owner
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Owner and reach
    content:
      key: owner
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Who owns it
        description: Who writes it and who sees it. Global and local are not values here; global is me everywhere, local is me in this repo.
        sections:
          - title: Org
            body: |
              Deployed by an admin to every machine or every account: a managed CLAUDE.md, managed-settings.json, skills provisioned through managed plugins or Settings > Skills. Empty on this PC.
          - title: Team
            body: |
              Committed in the repo, so everyone who clones it gets them: CLAUDE.md at an ancestor folder, the repo root, `.claude\CLAUDE.md` or a subfolder; `.claude\rules`; `.claude\skills`; `.claude\settings.json`; `.mcp.json`. In the chat app, a Project shared with people.
          - title: Me
            body: |
              My home folder for everywhere: `~\.claude\CLAUDE.md`, `~\.claude\rules`, `~\.claude\skills`, `~\.claude\settings.json`. Git-ignored files for one repo: `CLAUDE.local.md`, `.claude\settings.local.json`, and auto-memory under `~\.claude\projects\<key>\memory`. In the chat app, Settings > General, Settings > Memory, Settings > Skills, and a private Project.
  - key: reach
    label: The reach
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Owner and reach
    content:
      key: reach
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Where it applies
        description: How far a piece of configuration reaches. With owner it makes the cells that exist.
        sections:
          - title: Everywhere
            body: |
              Every project on this machine, or the whole account. In Claude Code this is the home folder, `~\.claude`. In the chat app it is the Settings pages.
          - title: A folder and everything below it
            body: |
              An ancestor folder, the repo root, or a subfolder are the same value at different depths. CLAUDE.md files stack along the path from the drive root down to the file being touched: `C:\Github\CLAUDE.md` applies to every repo under it, the repo's own CLAUDE.md to the repo, and `dates\CLAUDE.md` only when Claude works inside `dates`. A rule with a `paths:` list narrows reach further to matching files.
          - title: One Project
            body: |
              A claude.ai Project, in the chat app only: its instructions, knowledge files and memory space, for every chat anchored to it.
          - title: The cells that exist
            body: |
              | | Everywhere | A folder tree | One Project |
              |---|---|---|---|
              | Org | Managed CLAUDE.md, managed settings, provisioned skills | Nothing | A Project shared with the whole org, which is the team cell |
              | Team | Nothing | CLAUDE.md at any level, `.claude\rules`, `.claude\skills`, `.claude\settings.json`, `.mcp.json` | A shared Project |
              | Me | `~\.claude\CLAUDE.md`, `~\.claude\rules`, `~\.claude\skills`, `~\.claude\settings.json` | `CLAUDE.local.md`, `.claude\settings.local.json`, auto-memory | A private Project |

              The four populated Claude Code cells are the four folders of the claude-map mirror. Two gaps matter: there are no personal rules or skills for a single repo, and memory lives in exactly one cell, me in this repo, which is why it never reaches teammates or cloud sessions.
  - key: kind
    label: The kind
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Kinds
    content:
      key: kind
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What kind of thing it is
        description: Six kinds. Kind mostly decides when a thing loads, and settings is the only kind Claude never reads.
        sections:
          - title: Instruction
            description: Standing guidance on how to behave.
            body: |
              A CLAUDE.md in Claude Code: the user one, an ancestor folder's, the repo's, `.claude\CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, a subfolder's, or a file pulled in by an `@import` line. In the chat app, Settings > General or a Project's instructions field. Always in context for its reach, relevant or not, so it should be short and universal.
          - title: Rule
            description: An instruction in its own file, optionally scoped to paths. Claude Code only.
            body: |
              Every `.md` in `~\.claude\rules` or the repo's `.claude\rules` loads like an extra CLAUDE.md. A `paths:` list in its frontmatter makes it wait until Claude touches a matching file. Rule differs from instruction only in file layout and that optional filter.
          - title: Skill
            description: A procedure for one kind of task, with its scripts, templates and references.
            body: |
              From `~\.claude\skills`, the repo's `.claude\skills`, a plugin, the skills synced down from claude.ai, or Settings > Skills in the chat app. Only the description loads at start; the body loads when invoked, so it costs almost nothing until used.
          - title: Memory
            description: What Claude wrote down from earlier sessions.
            body: |
              Claude Code's auto-memory is a folder of markdown files under `~\.claude\projects\<key>\memory`, one per fact, with a MEMORY.md index. Machine-local, per repository, never shared. The chat app keeps memory in the account under Settings > Memory, with a space per Project. How memory is kept is a question of its own: the child book under this event.

              Two other things carry the name. A memory brief, such as orchestration's `claude\memory.brief`, is a curated memory for one repo. A `.memory` file, such as `Neogrids.memory`, is a Studio lens over a folder and holds no facts at all.
          - title: Knowledge files
            description: Reference material to consult, not guidance. Chat app only.
            body: |
              The documents uploaded into a Project: PDFs, spreadsheets, text, code. They say nothing about behaviour; they are the source of truth for a topic. The nearest Claude Code equivalent is the folder itself, the files Claude reads as it works, which is why a folder anchor and a Project anchor are partly substitutes.
          - title: Settings
            description: Permissions, hooks, environment, feature toggles. Enforced, never read as text.
            body: |
              Five tiers, highest precedence first: managed, command line, project local `.claude\settings.local.json`, shared project `.claude\settings.json`, user `~\.claude\settings.json`. Everything else in this book is context that Claude follows by judgment; settings are enforced by the harness. A hook is the one setting that puts text into context: whatever a SessionStart or UserPromptSubmit hook prints is added to the conversation, which is how orchestration's memory brief becomes memory.
  - key: memory
    label: How memory is kept
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Kinds
    when: [kind=memory]
    content:
      key: memory
      kind: playbook
      doc:
        version: 2
        title: How memory is kept
        description: The regime decides where memory lives and who sees it. The gate decides who controls what is written.
        decisions:
          - key: regime
            label: Which regime?
            values:
              - key: auto
                label: Auto-memory on
              - key: relocated
                label: Auto-memory relocated
              - key: brief
                label: Auto-memory off, a memory brief
              - key: account
                label: Account memory
          - key: gate
            label: Who decides what is written?
            values:
              - key: claude
                label: Claude writes on its own
              - key: ask
                label: Shown first, written only on a yes
              - key: enforced
                label: A hook or permission rule refuses the write
        events:
          - key: regime
            label: Where it lives and how it loads
            trigger: imposed
            hint: Answer which regime.
            content:
              key: regime
              kind: md
              by: [regime]
              docs:
                regime=auto: |
                  The default in Claude Code. `~\.claude\projects\<key>\memory`, keyed by the git repository so worktrees share it. The first 200 lines or 25KB of MEMORY.md load at start; topic files load when Claude opens them. Claude decides what to save. Machine-local: never shared with teammates or cloud sessions. The Neogrids regime.
                regime=relocated: |
                  `autoMemoryDirectory` in a settings file, honoured from any tier, moves the folder. Put it inside the repo, git-ignored, and the Studio shows the files like any other document. Everything else is as auto-memory on.
                regime=brief: |
                  `autoMemoryEnabled: false` in the repo's settings, no auto-memory folder at all, and a curated brief in the repo, one top section per area, one child per fact. A SessionStart hook injects the outline and a UserPromptSubmit hook the area names, so the brief is index at start, body on demand, reminder every prompt. It sits in the team cell: committed, shared, readable in the cloud. The orchestration regime.
                regime=account: |
                  Settings > Memory in the chat app, with a space per Project. Kept in the account, so it follows the user across devices. Whether a memory saved in a folder-anchored chat lands here or stays in the desktop app's database is still to verify after the merge.
          - key: gate
            label: What guards a write
            trigger: imposed
            hint: Answer who decides.
            content:
              key: gate
              kind: md
              by: [gate]
              docs:
                gate=claude: |
                  Claude saves what it judges useful for a future session, skipping what the codebase or a CLAUDE.md already says. Nothing to review until afterwards; `/memory` opens the files.
                gate=ask: |
                  A line in a CLAUDE.md or a rule such as "ask before saving a memory", or orchestration's playbook event "Something should be committed to memory": show the write, ask, write only on a yes, quote it in the report. Followed, not enforced.
                gate=enforced: |
                  A PreToolUse hook on writes under the memory folder, or the folder in the `ask` or `deny` permission list. The only hard guarantee; the hook can also log the write to a review queue instead of refusing it.
  - key: load
    label: The load tier
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Loading
    content:
      key: load
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: When it enters context
        description: Four tiers from eager to on demand. This is the axis the probes measure, and kind mostly decides it.
        sections:
          - title: At session start
            body: |
              Every CLAUDE.md in the chain, unscoped rules, skill descriptions, the MEMORY.md index, settings, and whatever a SessionStart hook prints. A CLAUDE.md of up to 4 MiB loads whole; the memory index only to 200 lines or 25KB. Paid every session whether relevant or not.
          - title: When a matching path is touched
            body: |
              A rule with a `paths:` list, and a subfolder's CLAUDE.md. Both stay out of context until Claude reads or edits a file the pattern or folder covers.
          - title: When invoked
            body: |
              A skill's body and the files beside it, by typing its name or by Claude choosing it from the description. A UserPromptSubmit hook's output arrives at every prompt the same way.
          - title: When Claude opens it
            body: |
              Memory topic files, the files in a folder, and Project knowledge: small totals load whole at chat start, large totals only as the passages a search matches. A fact buried in a memory topic file is invisible until Claude opens it.
          - title: What the probes showed
            body: |
              Loaded at start in both the terminal and the desktop app: the user CLAUDE.md, the parent folder's CLAUDE.md, the repo's CLAUDE.md, the `@import`, `.claude\CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, both unscoped rules, the memory index line, and both skill descriptions. Not loaded until triggered: both skill bodies, the memory topic file, both rules scoped to `dates\**`, and `dates\CLAUDE.md`. `/context` in a terminal session prints the same answer without probes.
